      "id": 1,
      "text": "Spencertown grew from a 1756 deed by the Mohican Indians to a New England settlement, earning the nickname “land of contention” during colonial border disputes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/spencertown_new_york",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Peter’s Church, originally a 1771 meeting house, now houses the Austerlitz Town Hall after its 2019 relocation into the former Methodist Episcopal building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/spencertown_new_york",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Austerlitz History Center, opened in a 1915 firehouse in Spencertown, displays the town’s story from Mohican times to the present.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
